From: Chiel Prins <chiel@cpeb.it>
Subject: [PATCH] Makefile: do not route CC through scripts/gcc-wrapper.py

The wrapper stops the build on any compiler warning whose exact
"file:line" is not in the allowed_warnings list hardcoded at the top of
it. That list was written against the compiler of the day; GCC 14 emits
221 warnings across a dozen kinds on this tree (-Waddress-of-packed-member,
-Warray-bounds, -Wpacked-not-aligned and so on), none of which it knows
about, so every one of them is fatal.

Extending the list is not workable: it is keyed on line numbers, so any
edit to an unrelated part of a file invalidates the entries after it.
Drop the wrapper instead. The warnings are still printed, they just no
longer abort the build.

Signed-off-by: Chiel Prins <chiel@cpeb.it>
---
--- a/Makefile	2026-09-18 17:17:37.088815739 +0200
+++ b/Makefile	2026-09-18 17:17:37.127171828 +0200
@@ -380,12 +380,6 @@
 PYTHON		= python
 CHECK		= sparse
 
-# Use the wrapper for the compiler. This wrapper scans for new
-# warnings and causes the build to stop upon encountering them.
-ifneq ($(wildcard $(srctree)/scripts/gcc-wrapper.py),)
-CC		= $(srctree)/scripts/gcc-wrapper.py $(CROSS_COMPILE)gcc
-endif
-
 CHECKFLAGS     := -D__linux__ -Dlinux -D__STDC__ -Dunix -D__unix__ \
 		  -Wbitwise -Wno-return-void $(CF)
 CFLAGS_MODULE   =
